---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';
const site = siteFromHost();
const path = "/compare/firepush-vs-omnisend";
const title = "Firepush vs Omnisend for Shopify: push specialist or channel hub?";
const desc = "Compare Firepush web push with Omnisend channel orchestration using browser reach, purchaser suppression, channel ownership and cost.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does more mobile traffic favor Firepush?",
    "answer": "Only if those devices can receive the intended notifications and shoppers grant permission. Measure supported and subscribed browsers separately from total mobile visits."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Firepush and Omnisend coexist?",
    "answer": "Potentially, with explicit channel ownership and tested purchase suppression. Do not enable duplicate cart reminders or assume the apps exchange every subscription and purchase state."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is push cheaper than SMS?",
    "answer": "Compare actual push send allowances, SMS destination charges and reachable audience. A low channel bill alone says nothing about incremental orders or the labor needed to coordinate senders."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
    <nav class="mb-8"><a class="link" href="/compare">All comparisons</a></nav>
    <header class="max-w-4xl"><p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p><h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">Choose around the job you need to add: a web push layer alongside an existing email sender, or a workflow combining email, SMS and push. Mobile traffic share alone does not establish that shoppers can receive browser notifications.</p></header>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Current scope matters more than old feature matrices</h2>
<p><a href="https://getfirepush.com/">Firepush’s current site</a> centers on Shopify web push, listing welcome, abandoned cart, price-drop and back-in-stock notifications. It describes paid push tiers by send volume. The <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/firepush">developer’s Shopify listing</a> also describes SMS. Confirm which channels can be activated in your account; neither source justifies treating Firepush as a complete email replacement.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.omnisend.com/features/automation/push-notifications/">Omnisend’s push overview</a> places browser notifications inside campaigns and automations. Its <a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/10485250-customize-workflow-channel-settings">channel-settings guide</a> describes email, SMS and push subscription handling in workflows. This makes Omnisend a candidate for combining channel logic, but having all channels in one interface does not establish better recovery performance.</p>
<h2>Reach audit before an opt-in experiment</h2>
<p>Break down visitors by browser and operating system, then test actual devices owned by the team. Inspect prompt visibility, subscription success and delivery after the store tab is closed. Count eligible visitors separately from everyone visiting the store; an opt-in percentage without that denominator can conceal a reach problem.</p>
<p><a href="https://support.omnisend.com/en/articles/2608314-get-started-with-push-notification-channel">Omnisend’s current setup guide</a> says its web push does not support iPhone browsers. Treat that as a product-specific planning restriction, not proof that all web push technology has the same limits. Obtain Firepush’s supported-device matrix and test it before estimating reach. A mobile-heavy audience is not automatically a push-addressable audience.</p>
<h2>Proposed trial: a limited product drop</h2>
<p>For a store that already has reliable email, build one Firepush product-drop notification and the equivalent Omnisend push message with the same destination and creative intent. This is a proposed exercise, not a reported merchant result. Preview long product names, an expired offer and a sold-out variant. Check whether a clicked notification opens the intended item and whether stale messages can be stopped.</p>
<p>Measure eligible visitors, permissions granted, delivered messages, clicks and completed orders separately. Browser subscriptions and email contacts are different units; do not divide push orders by the entire email list. If running a live controlled experiment later, allocate comparable eligible groups and avoid counting the same order as independent wins for push and email.</p>
<h2>Cart recovery with two owners</h2>
<table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Rehearsal</th><th>Evidence to retain</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Shopper buys while push is delayed</td><td>Order timestamp, automation exit and any queued notification</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shopper has email and push permissions</td><td>Which sender owns the next reminder and the frequency rule</td></tr>
<tr><td>Shopper revokes browser permission</td><td>Whether delivery stops and how the record is represented</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cart contains unavailable variant</td><td>Destination page and availability handling</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Firepush advertises stopping cart reminders after an order. Test the actual integration under a delayed purchase event. For a hybrid stack, identify how the email platform learns that push led to a purchase. Two dashboards require a shared journey map; do not assume they exchange suppression state merely because both connect to Shopify.</p>
<h2>Economics: cost per useful reachable audience</h2>
<p>Model pushes sent across campaigns and reminders, SMS destination and message units if used, and the existing email subscription. Ask whether retries and notification steps consume additional allowance. Omnisend consolidation also requires rebuilding the email flows you already own, so migration labor belongs in the decision.</p>
<p>Hypothetical labor example, not a provider quote: two extra hours per month coordinating separate senders at an assumed $50 per hour add $100 monthly operating cost. That can reverse a subscription saving. Replace both assumptions with observed trial effort; no universal push revenue share or opt-in threshold decides the purchase.</p>
<h2>When each architecture fits</h2>
<p>Evaluate Firepush as an additional layer when email is already maintained, a confirmed reachable push audience exists and product alerts have a distinct owner. Evaluate Omnisend when the objective is to rebuild channel sequencing in one workflow and the team accepts the migration effort. If most intended recipients cannot receive push, solve email or another permitted channel first.</p>
<p>Keep the old sender paused during controlled tests, retain the ability to stop each channel, and assign one recovery owner before launch. Related: <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-drip">Omnisend vs Drip</a> and the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/product-launch">product launch playbook</a>.</p>

</article></div></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Questions before choosing" />
</BaseLayout>
